---
/**
 * /privacy/ — deliberately minimal and factual.
 *
 * This exists because the dashboard now collects an email address, and
 * collecting one without saying what happens to it is not something we should
 * ship. It describes exactly what is collected today and nothing more; it is
 * not a general-purpose privacy policy and does not pretend to be. When the
 * Round 5 account layer stores addresses, this needs rewriting with a real
 * review (see HANDOFF.md Seam 4).
 */
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";

const title = "Privacy — What We Collect and Why | Texas Home Intelligence";
const description =
  "What Texas Home Intelligence collects, why, how long we keep it, and how to have it removed. " +
  "We collect an email address only when you ask to be told when the home dashboard launches.";
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ name: "Home", url: "/" }, { name: "Privacy", url: "/privacy/" }]} />

  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap" style="max-width:760px;">
      <span class="eyebrow">PRIVACY</span>
      <h1>What we collect and why</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Texas Home Intelligence publishes public data about areas, not about people. Reading
        anything on this site requires no account, and the data pages and ZIP dashboards store
        nothing about you.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap" style="max-width:760px;">
      <h2>What we collect</h2>
      <p>
        One thing: if you ask to be told when the home dashboard is ready, we store the
        <strong>email address you enter</strong> and the <strong>ZIP code you were viewing</strong>,
        along with a record of when you gave consent and which page you gave it on.
      </p>
      <p>
        We do not collect your name, street address, or phone number. We do not ask for them
        anywhere on this site today.
      </p>

      <h2>Why we collect it</h2>
      <p>
        To send you one message when the home dashboard launches. The ZIP tells us which metro to
        mention. That is the entire purpose.
      </p>

      <h2>Nothing is stored unless you consent</h2>
      <p>
        The signup form has a consent checkbox, and it is not decorative: the server refuses the
        submission without it, and the database itself rejects any record that does not carry
        consent. If you fill in the box and change your mind before ticking it, nothing about you
        reaches us.
      </p>

      <h2>We do not sell your data</h2>
      <p>
        We do not sell, rent, or share your email address with third parties, including
        contractors. We are not running an advertising business against it.
      </p>

      <h2>How to be removed</h2>
      <p>
        Email <a href="mailto:privacy@texashomeintelligence.com">privacy@texashomeintelligence.com</a>
        and ask, and we will delete your record. You do not have to explain why, and you do not
        need an account to ask.
      </p>

      <h2>Analytics</h2>
      <p>
        We use privacy-respecting page analytics to see which pages get read. We do not send your
        email address, your ZIP, or any other personal detail to an analytics provider.
      </p>

      <h2>What this page is not</h2>
      <p>
        This is a plain description of what the site does today, kept deliberately short so it
        stays accurate. It is not legal advice and not a comprehensive policy. As Texas Home
        Intelligence adds features that handle more of your information &mdash; a home profile, or
        alerts tied to an address &mdash; this page will be rewritten before those ship, not after.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
